---
import Solution from '../../layouts/Solution.astro';
import SolutionExample from '../../components/SolutionExample.astro';
import SolutionFit from '../../components/SolutionFit.astro';
import SolutionFaq from '../../components/SolutionFaq.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { solutionAreas } from '../../data/solutions';
import { askQuestionHref, contactEmail } from '../../data/contact';
import { products } from '../../data/products';
const area = solutionAreas.find((a) => a.key === 'review-changes')!;
const casework = products.find((p) => p.key === 'casework')!;
---
<Solution
  area={area}
  heading="Give each request for human review an owner and an accountable decision."
  description="Bring corrections, disputes and proposed changes into one review process that shows who is responsible and what is due. An approval records the decision, then a person or service with permission applies the change to the register."
>
  <SolutionExample
    eyebrow="One example: a licence scope correction"
    heading="Correct a licence with one officer accountable from request to decision."
    intro="A professional register receives a request to correct the scope of a licence. An officer needs to review the evidence, keep the request moving and record a decision before the licence changes."
    items={[
      { term: 'Who asks', body: 'The licence holder, or a service that spotted the wrong scope, sends a correction request to the register.' },
      { term: 'What staff need', body: 'One officer owns the request, its due date is visible to the team, and the licence remains unchanged until the approved correction is applied.' },
      { term: 'What happens', body: 'An officer reviews the request. If information is missing, the officer closes the review with changes requested. The application service accepts the holder’s revision and submits it for a new review. After approval, a person or service with permission applies the correction to the register.' },
      { term: 'What is kept', body: 'The application service keeps the holder’s current submission. Casework keeps each review assignment and decision. The register keeps the licence, who may change it and the history of the correction.' },
    ]}
    link={{ href: '/products/casework/#product-example', label: 'See the correction step by step' }}
  />
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="review-change-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changes for your office</p>
        <h2 id="review-change-title">Every review has an owner, while the register keeps control of record changes.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Your service defines which requests need review</h3><p>Your team decides which proposed changes require a person, such as a licence scope, a parcel relationship or a household member. Each kind of request can have its own queue, reviewer role and due date.</p></article>
        <article><h3>You can see who holds what, and what is overdue</h3><p>Each item has one officer and a due date. A supervisor sees which items have stalled and can chase them, and whoever asked can see the decision as soon as it is recorded; Casework itself sends no messages.</p></article>
        <article><h3>A review decision does not change the register by itself</h3><p>Casework records who reviewed and decided the request. Applying the resulting change requires an actor already authorised by the register, and the register records that action in its own history.</p></article>
      </div>
      <div id="what-your-office-calls-it" class="product-note">
        <h3>What your office calls it</h3>
        <p>Corrections and objections in a land register. Grievance redress in a social programme. Fitness-to-practise and disciplinary cases in a professional register. Appeals against a refused registration. Each is a request that needs a named officer, a due date and a record of who decided.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <SolutionFit
    area={area}
    heading="Connect each proposed change to the review it requires."
    muted
    jobs={{
      casework: 'Applies the review policy, assigns the request, tracks its due date and records the officer’s decision.',
      'base-registry': 'Holds the proposed change, sends it to the configured review authority and accepts the matching result before the change can be applied.',
    }}
    extra={[
      { term: 'Your officers keep deciding', body: 'The decision on each request stays with your officers and your rules. The queue tracks the work; it does not decide.' },
      { term: 'Screens for reviewers', body: 'Reviewers can use screens provided with the service or work through an existing staff application connected to Casework. They see the request, supporting material, due date and actions available to their role.' },
      { term: 'Requests from other systems', body: 'Casework works with Base Registry Engine as it comes. Another register system needs a connection your technical team builds, and any service can hand in a request and follow its progress.' },
    ]}
  >
    <p>Pair a Proposal Action with a Casework policy when a register change needs an accountable review. A proposal configured without review follows its register conditions and has no approval outcome.</p>
  </SolutionFit>
  <SolutionFaq
    items={[
      {
        q: 'Does this replace our register system?',
        a: 'No. Casework holds the work: who has each request, what is due, and who decided. Your register stays the authority on what a record says and who may change it.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/integration-patterns/', label: 'How it sits beside existing systems' },
      },
      {
        q: 'Can a request change the register without an officer?',
        a: 'For this reviewed flow, Casework records the officer’s decision. Base Registry Engine accepts only a matching result from the configured review authority, then checks the caller’s permission, the proposal conditions and that accepted result before applying the change. A different proposal explicitly configured without review has no officer approval outcome.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can other services send requests into the queue?',
        a: 'Yes. A service hands in a request, can follow its progress, and sees the decision when an officer records it. Base Registry Engine connects to Casework as it comes; another system needs an adapter written against Casework’s published contract.',
        link: { href: casework.tutorialHref, label: casework.tutorialLabel },
      },
      {
        q: 'Where does our technical team start?',
        a: 'The reviewed-change tutorial shows how to pair a Base Registry Engine Proposal Action with a Casework policy and configure Casework as its review authority. It is a configuration guide for the two products, rather than a complete application service.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/tutorials/review-breg-changes-in-casework/', label: 'Configure a reviewed register change' },
      },
    ]}
  />
  <DocsHandoff
    eyebrow="Work with us"
    heading="Talk it through with the people who build it."
    body="Tell us which requests need human review, who decides and how staff apply an approved change. We can show how the queue, reviewer screens and register can work together."
    primaryLabel="Ask a question"
    primaryHref={askQuestionHref(area.name)}
    emphasis="docs"
    note={`Or write to ${contactEmail}.`}
  />
</Solution>
